From 9743f741ebf2167df60f4a78af7fe8423f902dda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Danylo Storozhev <office@storozhev.net>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 15:14:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] Engram storage=cpu: load the pinned tables without a
 host-memory transient

Two things made `--engram-config '{"storage":"cpu"}'` OOM-kill the two
table-owning workers on a 278 GB host even though the tables are 2 x 101 GB:

1. torch's caching pinned-host allocator rounds every block up to a power
   of two, so torch.empty(..., pin_memory=True) for a 98.3 GB table takes a
   128 GiB pinned block (and 4 GiB for the 3.1 GB scales): 264 GiB for the
   pair. Allocate a plain CPU tensor at the exact size and page-lock it with
   cudaHostRegister instead (_pinned_exact). Registered memory is pinned and
   UVA-addressable exactly like cudaHostAlloc memory, so the existing
   get_cuda_view_from_cpu_tensor lookup path is unchanged.

2. The generic loader materialises each table in host RAM
   (safe_open().get_tensor copies the 98 GB tensor) before copy_() into the
   pinned parameter: ~200 GB per table at peak. Register the two tensor
   names as skipped (the iterator then also takes the no-mmap path for that
   shard, like storage=disk) and fill the pinned parameters straight from
   the shard after loading with chunked preadv (256 MiB, 8 threads), using
   the same header-based locator _DiskEngramTable uses. The model's
   load_weights runs the fill and marks the two parameters loaded.

Measured on the 8x CMP 170HX box (278 GB RAM): both tables read in 89 s and
98 s, steady MemAvailable 61 GiB, needles 3/3 at 128k. Against the NVMe
gather: 128k single-stream TTFT 21.3-23.5 s -> 17.3 s (6,066 tok/s prefill),
512k 88-106 s -> 78.7 s, 32k 8.1 s -> 5.8 s; decode at 128k 104-109 ->
117 tok/s.
